  "script": [
    {
      "beatId": "cache-front",
      "narration": "Every page load asks the Redis server first. It answers most reads from memory in well under a millisecond. When a key is missing, the focus moves to the database behind it, and the page waits."
    },
    {
      "beatId": "hash-table",
      "narration": "Inside the Redis server, every key lives in a hash table. A naive store would check each bucket in turn. Instead, the hash of the key points straight at bucket five, and that one stays marked."
    },
    {
      "beatId": "bucket-lock",
      "narration": "Slide over to a write. A writer grabs bucket five and locks it, so a second write has to wait its turn. The moment the first write finishes, the bucket is released and the second one goes through."
    },
    {
      "beatId": "memory",
      "narration": "Fade to memory. Usage climbs to ninety two percent. The gauge is marked and stays marked, while the eviction tag lights up only for a moment, then the focus shifts to the tag."
    },
    {
      "beatId": "cluster",
      "narration": "Zoom out, and one Redis server turns out to be one node of a cluster of three. Keys are split across the nodes. Then a question takes over the screen, and the cluster dims behind it."
    },
    {
      "beatId": "recap",
      "narration": "Cut back to the request path. The app asks the Redis server, the hash table finds the key in one step, and the database only hears about the rare miss."
    }
  ],
